 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Sydney rents are still rising, but much more slowly than at the peak. Prices are high after a big jump, and the squeeze is easing at the margin rather than reversing.\n\n**Rent inflation (ABS CPI, annual change)**\n- Sydney was at **3.5% in August 2026**. That is down from a peak of 8.8% in March 2024.\n- It has been stuck at about 3.3–3.5% since late 2025, so the slowdown has levelled off.\n- Sydney's overall CPI was about 3.9% over the same year (index 103.61 against 99.69 in August 2025). Rents are now growing slightly slower than prices generally.\n\n**How much rents have risen**\n- The monthly rent index went from 82.6 in July 2022 to 103.3 in August 2026, which is about **25% higher in four years**.\n- On the quarterly series, Sydney rents rose from 114.0 in Q4 2021 to 139.5 in Q3 2025, about **22% higher**.\n- Rents were falling slightly through 2020–21, then climbed from 2022.\n- The slowdown means rents are still rising each year, just not by as much. They are not falling.\n\n**Compared with other capitals (annual rent inflation, August 2026)**\n\n| City | Rent inflation |\n|---|---|\n| Perth | 5.3% |\n| Brisbane | 4.7% |\n| Adelaide | 4.1% |\n| Sydney | 3.5% |\n| Australia (eight-capital average) | 3.6% |\n| Melbourne | 2.5% |\n\nSydney is now near the national average. Perth and Brisbane are rising faster, and Melbourne is the softest.\n\n**Pressure on tenants**\n- NSW unemployment rose to **4.7% in September 2026**, from about 4.0–4.3% through 2025. A weaker job market makes the rent burden harder for some households.\n- Rents are growing at roughly the same pace as general prices, so they are no longer squeezing budgets more than everything else.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The CPI rent measure covers all existing tenancies, including long leases that reset slowly. Someone signing a new lease today may face larger increases than 3.5%. Asking rents and vacancy rates would show that, and I don't have that data here.\n- I haven't looked at wages, so I can't say whether incomes are keeping up with rents.",
